Fillets: the rounding of a closed circular edge (the rim of a boss) on a
cylinder whose seam is not one straight line (mitcad#337).

A join of two coaxial cylinders of one radius whose seams lie at different
angles (a boss extruded on in a second step) gives one cylindrical face
after the face merge, whose seam runs in steps: along the first part's
seam, along the circle between the parts to the second part's seam, and on
along that. The face is valid. A rounding of the boss's end circle is an
analytic blend (a torus) round a spine closed by its one edge, whose
parameters are the edge's, one period round it; its tangency line on the
cylinder is a line of the cylinder's parametric space, which
SplitKPart hatches with the face's boundary. Where the part the spine lies
on extends before the first part's seam in the face's parametric space
(the second seam at a smaller angle than the first), the hatching found
the face's domain a period before the blend's parameters; the spine's
parameters of that piece came out as half the spine, the rest was walked,
and the walk failed ("Path failed", ChFiDS_WalkingFailure).

- DomainsIntoPeriod, called by SplitKPart for a spine closed by its one
  edge: where the domains of a tangency line that is a line on a face
  periodic along it lie a whole period (or more) off the blend's
  parameters (their middle outside the period from the edge's first
  parameter), the line on the face is moved by those periods of the
  surface (the same points of it; checked against the surface's periods)
  and the domains with it, so that they lie in the blend's period.
  Domains across the start of the period (a straight seam with the
  spine's vertex off it) are left as they are.
